IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mc97-nouns`

-- MAGNA CARTA (1297), LAYER 1: CHAPTER 29, "Imprisonment, &c. contrary to Law. Administration of Justice."
-- Source: aep-Edw1cc1929-25-9-extract.md, the heading (line 34) and the text of chapter XXIX (line 35).
-- Two sentences.
--   The first forbids, to a Freeman, eight things said in the passive with no actor named (taken; imprisoned; disseised of
--   his Freehold, or Liberties, or free Customs; outlawed; exiled; any other wise destroyed) and two things done by "We"
--   (pass upon him; condemn him), "but by lawful judgment of his Peers, or by the Law of the Land".
--   The second says "We" will not sell, deny or defer Justice or Right, to any man.
-- Nothing here says the chapter is, or ever was, in force.
--
-- THE READINGS TAKEN (the register is in NOTES.md; each is marked here by its fork number):
--   F-C29-NEG    "nor will We not pass upon him, nor condemn him": the second "not" is read as adding no negation, so the
--                clause forbids passing upon and condemning, as the clauses before it forbid.
--   F-C29-SCOPE  "but by lawful judgment of his Peers, or by the Law of the Land" is read as qualifying EVERY limb of the
--                first sentence, the passive ones and the two done by "We".
--   F-C29-OR     the exception is two alternatives joined by "or": either one lifts the prohibition.
--   F-C29-ACTOR  the passive limbs name no actor, so no actor is invented: they are decision functions only.
--   F-C29-SELL   "sell" has no object in the text; the object of the neighbouring verbs ("Justice or Right") is taken.
--   F-C29-MAN    the last sentence says "any man" and "no man", not "Freeman"; the two sentences have different inputs.
--   F-C29-VAR    the variant "deal with him" for "condemn him" is NOT encoded; the text as printed is.

§ `Chapter 29: Imprisonment, &c. contrary to Law. Administration of Justice.`

§§ `The first sentence: what may be done to a Freeman`

-- gloss (not the text's): "Freeman", "lawful judgment of his Peers" and "the Law of the Land" are not defined by the text. The
-- asker says whether the person is a Freeman, whether there was a lawful judgment of his Peers, and whether it was done by the Law
-- of the Land. The two exception limbs are alternatives; either one makes the treatment "not forbidden by chapter 29", which is
-- NOT the same as lawful: the chapter forbids, and says nothing of what else may be needed.
-- src:35 | NO Freeman shall be taken or imprisoned, or be disseised of his Freehold, or Liberties, or free Customs, or be outlawed, or exiled, or any other wise destroyed;
-- src:35 | nor will We not pass upon him, nor condemn him,
-- src:35 | but by lawful judgment of his Peers, or by the Law of the Land.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), chapter 29, first sentence (src:35)
GIVEN `the person is a Freeman` IS A BOOLEAN
      treatment IS A Treatment
      `by lawful judgment of his Peers` IS A BOOLEAN
      `by the Law of the Land` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Chapter 29 outcome`
`chapter 29, first sentence: what is done to a Freeman` `the person is a Freeman` treatment `by lawful judgment of his Peers` `by the Law of the Land` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- not covered, silent: the sentence speaks of a Freeman ("NO Freeman shall be taken"), and of no one else.
        IF NOT (`the person is a Freeman`) THEN `not covered by chapter 29`
        -- not covered, unclosed list: the sentence lists what it lists; whether another treatment is "any other wise destroyed" is the asker's judgment (an undefined term).
        IF treatment EQUALS `some other treatment` THEN `not covered by chapter 29`
        -- not forbidden, by the text's own words: the exception "but by lawful judgment of his Peers, or by the Law of the Land" lifts the prohibition.
        IF `by lawful judgment of his Peers` OR `by the Law of the Land` THEN `not forbidden by chapter 29`
        OTHERWISE `forbidden by chapter 29`

-- Who the text names as doing the treatment: only "We", and only for the last two. For the eight passive limbs the answer is
-- NOTHING: the text names no one, and none is invented (F-C29-ACTOR).
-- src:35 | NO Freeman shall be taken or imprisoned, or be disseised of his Freehold, or Liberties, or free Customs, or be outlawed, or exiled, or any other wise destroyed;
-- src:35 | nor will We not pass upon him, nor condemn him,
@ref Magna Carta (1297), chapter 29, first sentence: who does it (src:35)
GIVEN treatment IS A Treatment
GIVETH A MAYBE Party
`chapter 29, first sentence: the party the text names as doing it` treatment MEANS
    CONSIDER treatment
        WHEN `passed upon` THEN JUST `the King`
        WHEN `condemned` THEN JUST `the King`
        -- NOTHING, silent: the eight passive limbs name no actor (F-C29-ACTOR).
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- THE REGULATIVE FORM of the two limbs done by "We". It is traced in mc97-tests-c29.l4, and it is NOT asserted: build 98 cannot
-- assert a DEONTIC (see NOTES.md, "What the stable binary does not accept"). The decision function above says the same thing
-- over the same facts and is asserted. The text gives no time limit, so there is no WITHIN: a prohibition with none stands.
-- The text gives no one who may enforce it, and no remedy: only BREACH is reported.
-- src:35 | nor will We not pass upon him, nor condemn him,
-- src:35 | but by lawful judgment of his Peers, or by the Law of the Land.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), chapter 29, first sentence: the King (src:35)
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`chapter 29, first sentence: the King shall not pass upon or condemn a Freeman` MEANS
    (PARTY `the King`
     SHANT `pass upon a Freeman` `the judgment given` `the law applied`
         PROVIDED NOT (`the judgment given` OR `the law applied`)
     HENCE FULFILLED
     LEST BREACH BY `the King` BECAUSE "chapter 29: passed upon a Freeman without lawful judgment of his Peers or the Law of the Land")
    RAND
    (PARTY `the King`
     SHANT `condemn a Freeman` `the judgment given` `the law applied`
         PROVIDED NOT (`the judgment given` OR `the law applied`)
     HENCE FULFILLED
     LEST BREACH BY `the King` BECAUSE "chapter 29: condemned a Freeman without lawful judgment of his Peers or the Law of the Land")

§§ `The last sentence: Justice or Right, to any man`

-- gloss (not the text's): "man" is not defined; the asker says whether the person is a man in the text's sense. No exception
-- is stated for this sentence, so the only answers are "forbidden" and "not covered". The three verbs (sell, deny, defer) all
-- lead to the same answer; the regulative rule below tells them apart.
-- src:35 | We will sell to no man, we will not deny or defer to any man either Justice or Right.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), chapter 29, last sentence (src:35)
GIVEN `the person is a man` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the thing dealt in` IS A `Thing dealt in`
GIVETH A `Chapter 29 outcome`
`chapter 29, last sentence: selling, denying or deferring` `the person is a man` `the thing dealt in` MEANS
    IF      `the person is a man`
        AND (`the thing dealt in` EQUALS Justice OR `the thing dealt in` EQUALS Right)
    THEN `forbidden by chapter 29`
    -- not covered, silent: the sentence names Justice and Right and "any man" (undefined); it says nothing of another person or another thing.
    ELSE `not covered by chapter 29`

-- THE REGULATIVE FORM of the last sentence; traced, not asserted. No time limit is stated, so there is no WITHIN.
-- src:35 | We will sell to no man, we will not deny or defer to any man either Justice or Right.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), chapter 29, last sentence: the King (src:35)
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`chapter 29, last sentence: the King shall not sell, deny or defer Justice or Right` MEANS
    PARTY `the King`
    SHANT `deal in Justice or Right` `the dealing` `the thing`
        PROVIDED `the thing` EQUALS Justice OR `the thing` EQUALS Right
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the King` BECAUSE "chapter 29: sold, denied or deferred Justice or Right"
